(2356406, 320822, 489, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is a very strong portrait to critique. My first impression is a very good one. On the technical side I think this is very well executed - I like the lighting, the skin tones are good, excellent use of DOF. The eye is sharp, as well as the lashes, which I really think is critical if you are going to zoom in this close. I also like the model''s expression for this image - almost undefinable, makes you wonder what she is thinking.<br/> <br/>My only suggestion on composition after looking at this for some time would be to crop in tighter on the right - essentially remove some of the negative space on the left. Not a lot, just a vertical strip from the left so that the curve of her shoulder meets the left lower corner. That still leaves a lot of negative space and at the same time I feel like it will pull her eye into the shot a little more and make it a bit more powerful. I''m not really sure that would work, just a suggestion there. The image is excellent as it stands. I see you had a lot of outakes posted and I like your choice of entry. 13th place is nothing to sneeze at after all :-)<br/> <br/>Looking forward to seeing more of your work.<br/> <br/>-Rich</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is a very strong portrait to critique. My first impression is a very good one. On the technical side I think this is very well executed - I like the lighting, the skin tones are good, excellent use of DOF. The eye is sharp, as well as the lashes, which I really think is critical if you are going to zoom in this close. I also like the model''s expression for this image - almost undefinable, makes you wonder what she is thinking. My only suggestion on composition after looking at this for some time would be to crop in tighter on the right - essentially remove some of the negative space on the left. Not a lot, just a vertical strip from the left so that the curve of her shoulder meets the left lower corner. That still leaves a lot of negative space and at the same time I feel like it will pull her eye into the shot a little more and make it a bit more powerful. I''m not really sure that would work, just a suggestion there. The image is excellent as it stands. I see you had a lot of outakes posted and I like your choice of entry. 13th place is nothing to sneeze at after all :-) Looking forward to seeing more of your work. -Rich', '2006-04-29 09:32:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356449, 321229, 35720, '<td valign="top">Congratulations Lárus. Great shot!</td>', 'Congratulations Lárus. Great shot!', '2006-04-29 09:42:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356453, 320822, 48968, '<td valign="top">I love this portrait! Her eye(s) are so huge and beautiful. What works for me is the fact that she is <i>not</i> smiling - and I really like the overall composition.</td>', 'I love this portrait! Her eye(s) are so huge and beautiful. What works for me is the fact that she is not smiling - and I really like the overall composition.', '2006-04-29 09:43:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356467, 321933, 489, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I see that you have already received a lot of very good feedback from both during and after challenge. SOme of what I will say here will echo some of these opinions I am sure.<br/> <br/>The good stuff that I see in this images is the composition and focus. Both look just fine to me. It would probably be worth experimenting with her looking a little bit more forward, but I like the concept. <br/> <br/>On the technical side, what it mainly boils down to is the lighting. The flash seems pretty harsh; there is a distinct shadow to the model''s left, especially near her chin where it is easy to spot. The pearl necklace is also reflecting far too much of the flash, the pearls look blown out in the center and become distracting. I like the pearls though, they add a lot to break up the darker elements.<br/> <br/>If you could try this again, but with natural light, I think you would have a dramatically different and much better shot. Fill flash could be used at very low power to get a catchlight if necessary. <br/> <br/>Take care, and keep shooting!<br/> <br/>Rich.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I see that you have already received a lot of very good feedback from both during and after challenge. SOme of what I will say here will echo some of these opinions I am sure. The good stuff that I see in this images is the composition and focus. Both look just fine to me. It would probably be worth experimenting with her looking a little bit more forward, but I like the concept.  On the technical side, what it mainly boils down to is the lighting. The flash seems pretty harsh; there is a distinct shadow to the model''s left, especially near her chin where it is easy to spot. The pearl necklace is also reflecting far too much of the flash, the pearls look blown out in the center and become distracting. I like the pearls though, they add a lot to break up the darker elements. If you could try this again, but with natural light, I think you would have a dramatically different and much better shot. Fill flash could be used at very low power to get a catchlight if necessary.  Take care, and keep shooting! Rich.', '2006-04-29 09:46:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356520, 321636, 11854, '<td valign="top">I thought this was very well done, I voted this an 8 during the challenge. I personally like the soft focus with kids (as you can see whenever I enter one). You generally tend to get banged for that here. But this is an adorable picture that you can treasure and that counts more than a score here.</td>', 'I thought this was very well done, I voted this an 8 during the challenge. I personally like the soft focus with kids (as you can see whenever I enter one). You generally tend to get banged for that here. But this is an adorable picture that you can treasure and that counts more than a score here.', '2006-04-29 10:05:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356613, 321878, 24073, '<td valign="top">An incredible shot, and I think it should have scored a bit higher in the challenge.  Maybe not a blue ribbon, because Inferno was awesome, but i think it was certainly worthy of a second or third.  A close second I might add, with the only thing holding this one back compared to inferno is the mood that they achieved using that devilish feeling woman.  I guess when you set a mood, or tone like that, using a woman just comes across as more dramatic.  Not to take anything away from the subject here, because its obviously a good-looking fella, and he nailed the look to a T.<br/>    I would be interested in hearing why "you" thought Inferno outscored this shot?</td>', 'An incredible shot, and I think it should have scored a bit higher in the challenge.  Maybe not a blue ribbon, because Inferno was awesome, but i think it was certainly worthy of a second or third.  A close second I might add, with the only thing holding this one back compared to inferno is the mood that they achieved using that devilish feeling woman.  I guess when you set a mood, or tone like that, using a woman just comes across as more dramatic.  Not to take anything away from the subject here, because its obviously a good-looking fella, and he nailed the look to a T.    I would be interested in hearing why "you" thought Inferno outscored this shot?', '2006-04-29 10:26:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356893, 321833, 45524, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique club. I think this is a well done and nicely posed and framed photo. The antique and painting feel of this works extremely well. The coloring of the photo seems extremely lifelike and well done. There are a few things I think could be changed to make it score even better then it did. Although the colors are nice the photo seems a bit flat bumping the contrast up with levels and curves adjustment would make the photo POP which seems to be required to score in the top 10 here on DPC. Focus on the face is a touch soft. The antique feel while wonderful in my book seems to be working against you with the voters. The other thing that I can think of is that its not truly a studio Portrait but more of a well done snapshot. Had this been in a different challenge I think it would have scored even better and placed better as well. Overall well done and a good placement considering the challenge.</td>', 'Hello from the Critique club. I think this is a well done and nicely posed and framed photo. The antique and painting feel of this works extremely well. The coloring of the photo seems extremely lifelike and well done. There are a few things I think could be changed to make it score even better then it did. Although the colors are nice the photo seems a bit flat bumping the contrast up with levels and curves adjustment would make the photo POP which seems to be required to score in the top 10 here on DPC. Focus on the face is a touch soft. The antique feel while wonderful in my book seems to be working against you with the voters. The other thing that I can think of is that its not truly a studio Portrait but more of a well done snapshot. Had this been in a different challenge I think it would have scored even better and placed better as well. Overall well done and a good placement considering the challenge.', '2006-04-29 11:33:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2356943, 321807, 45524, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique club. First let me tell you what I love about this photo. I like composition of this photo. I love the models expression and the framing of her hands for the face. The shallow DOF works well for the right side of the photo, however getting a little deeper DOF and sharper focus on the eyes would make this photo stand out. I also feel that the hair in front of the right eye is distracting. Overall a pretty good attempt with a few changes this could be a very well done shot.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique club. First let me tell you what I love about this photo. I like composition of this photo. I love the models expression and the framing of her hands for the face. The shallow DOF works well for the right side of the photo, however getting a little deeper DOF and sharper focus on the eyes would make this photo stand out. I also feel that the hair in front of the right eye is distracting. Overall a pretty good attempt with a few changes this could be a very well done shot.', '2006-04-29 11:48:11.000000', NULL, 0),
